            <ArticleTitle>The effects of a high glycerin content hydrogel premolded mask dressing on post-laser resurfacing wounds.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>162-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/14764172.2011.594063</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Laser resurfacing for the rejuvenation of facial skin remains a popular cosmetic procedure. Postoperative care for laser resurfacing is important to optimize healing, reduce pain and minimize complications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the efficacy of the new dressing against placebo (Vaseline® cream) after Er:YAG laser resurfacing.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Fifteen patients between 45 and 72 years of age with facial wrinkles were enrolled in the study. Patients underwent full-face Er:YAG procedures. The wounds were then dressed: one side of the face was treated with Vaseline and the other with a hydrogel dressing. Erythema, edema, pigmentation, average time of re-epithelization, pain score, itching, clinical evaluation of infection, crust formation and acidity were documented.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The hydrogel dressing decreased postoperative morbidity. The site treated by hydrogel showed a shorter epithelial healing time than the Vaseline site. The dressing relieved the immediate pain of facial resurfacing as well as preventing crust formation and itching.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A hydrogel dressing is a better and suitable alternative to the open technique to manage post-laser wound healing.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
